---
// existing imports
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

// helper to build hrefs for local files (prefixes BASE_URL and encodes)
const siteBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL ?? "/";
const mkHref = (p) => {
  if (!p) return p;
  if (p.match(/^https?:\/\//)) return p;
  const cleaned = p.replace(/^\/+/, ""); // remove leading slash if any
  return `${siteBase}${encodeURI(cleaned)}`;
};
---

<MainLayout title="Research — Federico D'Onofrio">

  <h1>Research</h1>

  <section>
    <h2 class="section-title">Policy action and collective decision-making</h2>

    <p>
      A common thread running through my research is an interest in
      <strong> policy action and collective decision-making</strong>: how
      collective actors come to understand a situation, define what ought
      to be done, and act upon it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Collective decision-making is not simply the spontaneous aggregation
      of individual choices, but a process that requires forms of knowledge,
      expertise and judgement. While the literature has so far underlined
      the importance of models and other forms of representation as part of
      the "art of world-making", my principal concern is somewhat different:
      <strong> how information, expertise and values are brought together
      in processes of collective decision-making and policy action.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      Collective decisions depend, first of all, on the ability to
      <strong> understand particular situations</strong>. Much of my research
      has therefore examined the production and use of information: the ways
      in which economic and social conditions are observed, measured and
      represented, and the role played by different forms of data and by
      the experts who produce and interpret them. This concern is central
      to my work on the history of agricultural information, including
      <em> Observing Agriculture</em>
      <a href="https://www.routledge.com/Observing-Agriculture-in-Early-Twentieth-Century-Italy-Agricultural-economists-and-statistics/DOnofrio/p/book/9781032402482">[--&gt;]</a>,
      my research on Wolf Ladejinsky
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1215/00182702-9779614">[--&gt;]</a>,
      and my work on accounting offices in Switzerland and the Netherlands.
    </p>

    <p>
      At the same time, information does not by itself determine a course
      of action. Collective decision-making also requires the identification
      of <strong>goals and values</strong>: decisions depend on what actors
      understand to be desirable, important or worth pursuing. This dimension
      has informed my work on the purposes of economic policy and, more
      recently, my research on the value of accounting, including the
      forthcoming special issue on the subject and the conference I
      co-organised with Harro Maas in Lausanne in 2024.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section>
    <h2 class="section-title">Agriculture</h2>

    <p>
      A major part of my research has focused on
      <strong> the production of knowledge for agricultural policy</strong>,
      and on the different empirical practices, organisations and forms of
      expertise through which European agriculture was made observable.
    </p>

    <p>
      In <em>Observing Agriculture in Early Twentieth-Century Italy</em>
      <a href="https://www.routledge.com/Observing-Agriculture-in-Early-Twentieth-Century-Italy-Agricultural-economists-and-statistics/DOnofrio/p/book/9781032402482">[--&gt;]</a>,
      I examined three principal modes through which agriculture was
      investigated and represented:
      <strong> enquiries, statistics and monographs</strong>.
      My subsequent research has extended this analysis in several directions.
    </p>

    <p>
      One concerns <strong>accountancy data</strong>, which became central
      to my work through FARMACCOUNTA
      <a href="/farmaccounta">[--&gt;]</a>
      and subsequently DATAREV
      <a href="https://datarev.univie.ac.at/">[--&gt;]</a>.
      This work also resulted in the volume
      <em> Farm Accounts in Rural Europe, c. 1700–1914</em>
      <a href="https://boydellandbrewer.com/book/farm-accounts-in-rural-europe-c-1700-1914-hb/">[--&gt;]</a>,
      edited with Nathalie Joly <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Nathalie-Joly/3">[--&gt;]</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another concerns <strong>field observation and field trips</strong>,
      explored in my work on
      Wolf Ladejinsky
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1215/00182702-9779614">[--&gt;]</a>.
      A third concerns the development of
      <strong> international agricultural statistics</strong>, particularly
      through my research on the International Institute of Agriculture
      with Niccolò Mignemi:
      <em>Making Perfect Markets</em>
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1353/jwh.2025.a974184">[--&gt;]</a>
      and
      <em>The International Institute of Agriculture and the Information
      Infrastructure of World Trade</em>
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.4000/histoiremesure.19003">[--&gt;]</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Taken together, these strands have led me to describe the emergence
      of a <strong>"first data revolution in European agriculture"</strong>:
      a transformation in the ways agricultural economies could be observed,
      compared and represented, and consequently in the possibilities for
      collective action in the agricultural field.
    </p>

    <p>
      This research has also led me to consider the
      <strong> organisations that circulated agricultural knowledge and steeered agricultural modernization</strong>. I have examined both intergovernmental
      organisations and non-governmental organisations, particularly the
      agricultural accounting offices studied in my work on Switzerland
      and the Netherlands.
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally, I have increasingly turned from the forms and institutions
      of agricultural knowledge to the <strong>experts themselves and the
      construction of expertise</strong>: how particular individuals and
      professional communities acquired authority, how their knowledge was
      established and circulated, and how expertise came to play a role in
      agricultural policy  <a href={mkHref("papers/rise-of-agricultural-economics.pdf")} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[-->]</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In July 2026, I organised the conference
      <strong> Two Centuries of Agricultural Futures: Actors and Networks
      of Agricultural Policy-Making in Europe from the 1760s to the 1960s </strong>
      in Bologna, together with
      Myriam Lichani
      <a href="/people#myriam-lichani">[--&gt;]</a>
      and Filippo Soramel
      <a href="/people#filippo-soramel">[--&gt;]</a>.
      <a href={mkHref("conferences/datarev-workshop_two-centuries-of-agricultural-futures.pdf")} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"> Conference programme and poster [-->]</a>
    </p>
  </section>


  <section>
    <h2 class="section-title">The rise of political economy</h2>

    <p>
      At the origins of political economy, in the eighteenth century, lies
      a concrete concern with <strong>government action</strong>. The
      Neapolitan tradition of <em>Economia civile</em> articulated this
      concern in terms of
      <em> felicitas publica</em>
      <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/S1053837215000401 ">[--&gt;]</a>
      and of the
      <strong> functions of government</strong>
      <a href="https://www.francoangeli.it/rivista/getArticoloFree/62420/En">[--&gt;]</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Policy action, in this context, depended on the construction of an
      <strong> information space</strong> that was complex and articulated.
      My research on the administrative culture of Ancien Régime Naples
      has examined the conflicts surrounding the production, circulation
      and authority of information, particularly in relation to grain policy,
      in <em>"Numeri falsi e numeri errati: le basi informative della politica granaria a Napoli nella seconda metà del secolo XVIII</em>
      <a href="https://cris.unibo.it/handle/11585/1031705">[--&gt;]</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      My current research takes this question further by investigating the
      emergence, in the eighteenth century, of a distinctive attitude towards
      <strong> facts</strong>. Much of the existing literature has explored
      the relationship between political economy and the natural sciences,
      particularly Newtonian physics. My research instead explores the
      connections between economic knowledge and the
      <strong> antiquarian and philological traditions</strong>, asking how
      these traditions contributed to the emergence of what could count as
      an economic fact.
    </p>

    <p>
      This research is developed further in
      <em> Economics and the Antiquarian: What is an Economic Fact in the
      18th Century?</em>
      <a href={mkHref("papers/ESHET-2026-paper.pdf")} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">[-->]</a>,
      a paper presented at the HES-ESHET conference in Nice in 2026 and
      during my visiting professorship at EHESS.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section>
    <h2 class="section-title">New research directions</h2>

    <h3>European industrial policy</h3>

    <p>
      Most work on the economic constitution of European institutions has
      focused on the creation of the <strong>Common Market and Single Market</strong>,
      and on the <strong>Economic and Monetary Union</strong>. My new research
      takes a different approach by examining more closely the developmental
      ambitions of European institutions and the ways in which they sought
      to shape investment and economic transformation.
    </p>

    <p>
      I am particularly interested in the <strong>investment priorities </strong>
      identified by European organisations: first through the activities of
      the <strong>European Investment Bank</strong>, and subsequently through
      initiatives such as the Trans-European Transport Network
      (TEN-T) and European regional policies.
    </p>


    <h3><em>Coup d'œil</em></h3>

    <p>
      A further line of research examines the early-modern concept of
      <em> coup d'œil</em> as a way of understanding 
      <strong> decision-making and practical judgement</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>
        “Coup d’Oeil: An early-modern concept to understand
        decision-making”
      </em>
      <a href="/coup-doeil">[--&gt;]</a>
      — presented at the 2019 conference of the British Society for the
      History of Science in York and at
      <em> Dancing diagrams, numbers, and other things</em> workshop in Lausanne in 2024.
    </p>
    <h3>Backwardness</h3>

<p>
  A further line of research examines the history of
  <strong> backwardness</strong>: how the modern concept emerged and became
  an object of policy intervention. Critical approaches to development
  studies have traditionally understood backwardness as the result of a
  foreign gaze upon an organic community, whether a village or a nation.
  My research instead explores how metrics of progress and backwardness
  may be constructed <strong>within the community itself</strong>, and how
  they can support strategies of catching up through reform, emulation and
  adaptation. Backwardness thus becomes not simply an external judgement,
  but an element in the political strategies of historical actors themselves.
</p>
  </section>

</MainLayout>